dd-416a-506b-9085-7936c937e72b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186560766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186560766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560958 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dca6600a-f1ed-5820-b07a-a9d181dedbc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y={x}^{2}+4x-11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b6f37ac-511e-531f-9fd9-0e1aa3bfef93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560972 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$ and $b=4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{4}{2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-2$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+4x-11 \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+4(-2)-11 \\\\  & =4-8-11 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-2,-15).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5f/5f76e31fbf6e2d7b1398eaafc97e634e955b3314697d650d5f645555c1df289e.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=3{x}^{2}+kx+2$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ea1e232-4156-584c-babd-20b14e2a349e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560988 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ we will set our result equal to $2.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=2,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{6} & =2 \\\\ k & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=-3{x}^{2}-18x+7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f0e7d2f-2da0-5cf5-aa62-0f9950fe080c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186560993 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=-3$ and $b=-18$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-18)}{2(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-18)}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-6x+7?$\n\nThe coordinates of the vertex are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "621c054c-a8ee-5e21-b7c5-55a43d66b230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561003 #edb/ref 1074 "The parabola's vertex lies on the symmetry axis, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$ and $b=-6$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}-6x+7 \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-6(3)+7 \\\\  & =9-18+7 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9f/9fd54a6b5f5c5c4a711dbdc7de7fcea84177454d6f4a04a1fd647e6a9d93b904.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=-3{x}^{2}+kx+5,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7198fc6e-53bf-58ce-9c21-b096160c3a27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561014 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-1,$ we will set our result equal to $-1.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-3$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{(-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-1,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{6} & =-1 \\\\ k & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+8x-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a249418-3166-5952-aa2e-ffdd779176d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561017 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=2$ and $b=8$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{8}{2(2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry for the parabola $y={x}^{2}-4x-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89d5541c-c810-5b48-ae7e-160f63479682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561024 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=1$ and $b=-4$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{(-4)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52326" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}+kx-2$ is $x=-1.$ Find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a434264-115c-5e23-9940-79d2727ed021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561025 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, we will set our result equal to $-1.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=4$ and $b=k$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{k}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-1,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{8} & =-1 \\\\ -k & =-8 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5cb63eb200d809aa94800fc7990c9a621ad2aca32d86cb1aeb39c844eb9c8205.png)\n\nFind the value of $k$ for the parabola $y=k{x}^{2}+12x-6,$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8de5ec05-717f-57d0-aca0-bdf66baf299d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561028 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since the graph tells us that the axis of symmetry is $x=-3,$ we will set our result equal to $-3.$ \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=k$ and $b=12$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{12}{2(k)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{2k} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-3,$ so we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{k} & =-3 \\\\ -6 & =-3k \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-13386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=3{x}^{2}+36x+6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9df948ff-fcd8-5687-95bb-a0bdf3f05629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561032 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=36$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{36}{2(3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{36}{6} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-6.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=-6$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}+36x+6 \\\\  & =3(-6)^{2}+36(-6)+6 \\\\  & =108-216+6 \\\\  & =-102\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(-6,-102).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $y=-5{x}^{2}+30x+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4514a54-7bd5-5a89-8cc0-e28359cd784d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561041 #edb/ref 1074 "The vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so we will start by computing the axis of symmetry. \n\nThe equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=-5$ and $b=30$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{30}{2(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{30}{(-10)} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=3.$ This also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we substitute $x=3$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5{x}^{2}+30x+2 \\\\  & =-5(3)^{2}+30(3)+2 \\\\  & =-45+90+2 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(3,47).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-222076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=4{x}^{2}+3x+2$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff16fd72-aa33-5ed0-9859-414065a202fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561065 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a=4$ and $b=3$ into the formula for the axis of symmetry. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{2(4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{3}{8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
